Let $E_1$ be the events that man will be selected and $E_2$ the events that woman will be selected.
Then, $P\left(E_1\right)=\frac{1}{4}\begin{array}{ll}
\text { So, } & P\left(\bar{E}_1\right)=1-\frac{1}{4}=\frac{3}{4} \\
\text { and } & P\left(E_2\right)=\frac{1}{3} \\
\Rightarrow & P\left(\bar{E}_2\right)=\frac{2}{3}
\end{array}
$\therefore \quad E_1$ and $E_2$ are independent events.
$\begin{aligned}
\therefore \quad P\left(\bar{E}_1 \cap \bar{E}_2\right) & =P\left(\bar{E}_1\right) \times P\left(\bar{E}_2\right) \\
& =\frac{3}{4} \times \frac{2}{3}=\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}

